# Differences and review boundaries

Read sourceCommit and engineCommit in HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json, which is
generated at export, for the exact package and engine source. This register
describes known differences in the source; it is not a comparison result in
the four categories (persistence, semantics, rendering, side effects), which
must be measured separately. The reference is Ruby Decko 0.20.0 / card 1.110.0
only; the reference runtime is Ruby 3.2.3 on the project's Linux VM, where
the synthetic-v1 recorded Ruby outputs were captured on 2026-11-03, and
EVIDENCE.md states the reference each quoted result used.

The TypeScript implementation is a port with host and storage abstractions,
not a directory mirror or a Ruby interpreter replacement. This register names
the known classes of difference; it does not assert that no other defect exists.
RUBY-MAP.md, generated at export, also lists the source lines that match its
documented difference-marker rule (a divergence word stem, "unported", "not
supported", or "deliberate" or "intentional" followed by "differ" on the same
line). The shipped files have their comments removed, so these line references point
to the development source (sourceCommit in HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json), which
this package does not include, not to lines of the shipped files. Those
references preserve a review trail without presenting source comments as
accepted exceptions or treating unmeasured differences as compatible
behavior.

| Area | Difference and reason | Source to inspect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Name algebra | ActiveSupport inflection tables and Cardname behavior are ported to TypeScript; runtime and encoding details need captured comparisons. | src/card/name/ |
| Rendering | Views are TypeScript functions and registered mod chains rather than Ruby set-module/HAML methods. This permits execution on JavaScript hosts; arbitrary Ruby view extensions need their own compatibility proof. | src/card/render/, src/card/mod-loader/ |
| SQL | Driver interfaces replace ActiveRecord. D1 uses a compensating undo journal and a leased lock rather than an interactive transaction; the journal covers the save tables, not every table in the database. | src/server/driver.ts, src/server/drivers/ |
| Unknown outcomes | A lost mutation or COMMIT acknowledgement is reported as an unknown outcome with retry forbidden; it is not described as a rollback. Pending integration effects and retained file backups require reconciliation against stored card/action history. This avoids duplicate writes and removing files that a committed row may reference. | src/card/actions/save-outcome.ts, src/server/drivers/ |
| File recovery | Conditional restoration is available only where the store binds comparison to replacement. Memory and the R2 adapter implement byte-based comparison; filesystem and the configured HTTP store for the hosted file storage of the Bunny (bunny.net) edge platform expose an unavailable-capability result. Byte equality does not distinguish upload epochs or metadata-only rewrites. This protects later writers and preserves backups on ambiguity. | src/card/attachments/attachment-store.ts, src/blob/ |
| IDs and rollback | Some adapters do not reuse rolled-back IDs; orphan-history avoidance can allocate beyond IDs in history. The native MySQL main-fixture grade now matches recorded rows across its 16 persistence comparisons, but that does not establish arbitrary rollback or mod-event row behavior. The remaining mod-write failures still require repair. | src/card/history/write-path.ts; EVIDENCE.md |
| Revision reverts | The history pane's revert refuses some revisions that Ruby's rollback applies: restoring another card of the act from the trash when its revision also changes that card's content, type or parts; changing another card that is in the trash or whose name cannot be resolved; and changing which parts (left or right) a compound card is made of. The port has no recorded Ruby result for these shapes. The request is refused with a validation error (422) naming the card ("... is not supported"), and nothing is stored. | src/card/actions/card-action-engine.ts (revertActions) |
| Credentials | The port accepts legacy hashes and supports PBKDF2 upgrades. Ruby cannot verify upgraded PBKDF2 records; legacy-sha1 policy is needed for a shared Ruby deck or rollback requiring Ruby sign-in. | src/card/auth/password-hash.ts, src/server/same-database.ts |
| Sessions and secrets | Digest storage, constant-time comparisons, sign-in throttling and credential-linked revocation deliberately harden account handling. These can change error messages and session lifetime. | src/card/auth/ |
| Browser writes | Origin checks and the first-run setup token restrict browser writes and owner creation. External clients must satisfy the configured policy. | src/server/request-origin.ts, src/server/first-run-routes.ts |
| Security corrections | Some security-related differences require coordinated private disclosure. This package makes no promise to copy upstream vulnerabilities; details and reproduction payloads are not asserted to be a complete public disclosure. | account and permission source; independent private review |
| Jobs and mail | Host adapters provide scheduling/execution and mail transport. Declared hooks alone do not prove delivered mail, delayed execution or recovery after a process failure. | src/card/delayed-job/, src/card/notify/, src/server/edge-delayed-work.ts |
| Notification links | The notification mail's unsubscribe link carries the reference's bytes in every configuration: the port posts no success envelope of its own, as the reference's view does not. By default a click is refused as the reference refuses it (403, nothing applied). With DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_MAIL_UNFOLLOW_REFUSAL=off the click instead answers the hard redirect (303) the reference's response.rb gives a permitted write, and the port's own dashboard write lands. | src/card/notify/follow-mail-views.ts; tests/card/notify/notification-unfollow-link-parity.test.ts; KNOWN-COMPARISON-FAILURE.md |
| Main and nested render errors | Main-page errors and explicit focal errors rethrow even in production, as Ruby does. Nonfocal bare-Date validation now returns the exact Ruby error span; nested and global-loud controls remain passing. The missing email-template codename is reproduced as a real error rather than converted into a page. | src/card/render/page.ts, src/card/render/format.ts; EVIDENCE.md |
| Remaining mod writes | The complete mod probe still fails persistence, side-effect and non-card-table comparisons. Generated-field writes, authorization decisions and error responses remain under repair; these failures are not accepted compatibility exceptions. Passing view comparisons does not complete them. | src/card/actions/; EVIDENCE.md |
| Recorded sign-in messages | Default answers use uniform failure wording. DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_SIGNIN_DETAILED_ERRORS=on selects the recorded per-field wording. This is a separate axis from session storage and does not prove resistance to timing attacks. | src/card/parity/strict-parity-flags.ts |
| Recorded revision crash | Default File/Image revision restoration proceeds; DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_REVISION_RESTORE_500=on selects the recorded crash response without a stored change. This preserves an explicitly selectable reference path rather than making every deployment crash. | src/card/parity/strict-parity-flags.ts |
| Recorded skin crash | Default invalid skin-parent handling refuses with validation errors; DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_SKIN_CREATE_500=on selects the recorded crash response. Whole-page layout and status require their own comparison. | src/card/parity/strict-parity-flags.ts |
| Compiled asset names | Default bundle names derive from content. DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_SCRIPT_BUNDLE_CLOCK_NAMES=on selects Ruby's wall-clock-second stand-in action ID, including possible repeated names within one second. | src/card/parity/strict-parity-flags.ts |
| Mail follow-rule refusal | DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_MAIL_UNFOLLOW_REFUSAL defaults on and selects the measured dashboard refusal and follow-rule refusal path; an explicit off keeps the port's own unfollow behaviour. The notification link is the bare recorded one in both settings. The five strict switches are independent; the other four default off, and none is a universal compatibility switch. | src/card/parity/strict-parity-flags.ts |
| Mod helper shape | Declared synchronous format helpers and permission predicates use the TypeScript declaration interface. Undeclared helpers raise a TypeScript declaration diagnostic, async helpers are refused, and trait declarations do not automatically generate all Ruby accessor methods. | src/card/mod-loader/mod-dsl.ts, src/card/mod-loader.ts |
| Host configuration | Deck profile, serializer era, public shell, asset configuration and origin are independent inputs. Selecting strict behavior flags does not supply an old site's content, media or deployment configuration. | configured-public-page-shell.mjs, src/server/deployment-legacy-assets.ts, src/server/deployment-deck-origin.ts |
| Files and assets | Blob adapters and vendored compiler inputs replace Ruby filesystem/asset tooling. File identifiers, rendition timing and source-byte licensing need separate inspection. | src/card/attachments/, src/card/assets/, src/blob/, vendor/ |
| Stylesheet content | Saving a card that feeds a stylesheet (a CSS, SCSS, skin or Bootswatch skin card, or another stylesheet input) is refused as an invalid card (422, with the saved card's content error, the way Ruby reports a stylesheet syntax error) when the stylesheet content contains a nest (`{{...}}`), an escaped link (`\[[...]]`) or a link to a card by name; it is refused with a server error (500) when a skin lists a list card (a pointer, list, link list, nest list or skin) as an item. Ruby renders nests and card links through their views and expands list items. Links to a deck path beginning with `/` and to `http`, `https` or `mailto` addresses are rendered following Ruby's CSS-format link rendering. | src/card/assets/skin-stylesheet.ts, src/card/actions/style-asset-events.ts |
| Runtime abstraction | JavaScript host interfaces replace Rails controllers and Ruby process state. This is not a second complete first-class Ruby runtime implementation. | src/server/, vm-staging-server.mjs |

Performance, energy and hosting cost are not measured by the package smoke
check. No replacement claim is made for historical figures. Full fidelity for
arbitrary decks, mods and backends remains a behavioral acceptance requirement,
not something established by the presence of these files.

A comparison of a deployed port with a running Ruby Decko site used as the
comparison reference requires `DECKO_SERIALIZER_ERA=live` and
`DECKO_HAML_SLOT_STYLE=hash`; see [DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md). With Ruby's
view cache on, the context Ruby leaves in a cached view can depend on which
requests came earlier. The port does not reproduce that behavior. A match on
views rendered with an empty cache, or a match with Ruby's view cache turned
off, does not show that the port matches Ruby with the cache on.
